entries.body:
`PgPool` runs many PostgreSQL connections behind one sans-IO interface, so a program's own nish:net loop serves HTTP and queries together without blocking.

The pool's size is a cap, and each slot's connection is built on first use. `dispatch()` gives a request to the least-busy ready connection, never more than `maxInFlight` groups deep on one, and queues the rest FIFO in a bounded queue. Statements registered once are prepared on each connection in the same write as its first request. Each group's ReadyForQuery is its commit point, so an error such as a 23505 fails only its own group.

A connection that fails reports each of its outstanding requests as `PG_LOST`, oldest first, then `PG_FAILED`, and is rebuilt on demand. Every failure path wipes that connection's copy of the password. A full queue, an over-deep pipeline or an out-of-range slot is a defined refusal or panic, never unbounded growth.

tests/postgres/carrier.ts is the carrier: an HTTP server and the pool on one epoll loop, built on tcpConnect, pollAdd and pollWait, that answers HTTP while a query is in flight.

tests/postgres/live runs the client against PostgreSQL 18 over SCRAM-SHA-256:
- the Conduit article list, pipelined, printing spec-format rows;
- 100 groups on 4 connections, at most 16 deep, where the 4 groups that hit a 23505 commit nothing and the other 96 commit and read back;
- 28P01 on a wrong password;
- a server that resets mid-startup;
- `pg_terminate_backend` on a live connection.

A new `postgres-live` CI job runs it on a `postgres:18` service container.

The Conduit schema and article-list query come from arjaythedev/multicore-server-challenge (MIT) and are listed in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. docs/security/postgres.md records what a hostile server can make the pool do: discard one queued request per failed connect, and hold about 3 × `maxMessage` per slot. It also records the program's duties: a fresh nonce per connection, and owning the socket's lifetime.

bench/postgres measures nish/net/postgres's client CPU per query against libpq 18 in pipeline mode, on PostgreSQL 18 with the multicore server challenge's --dev seed. bench/postgres/setup.sh builds the cluster and seed from the challenge at a pinned commit.

The workloads:
- a primary-key SELECT 16 deep;
- the Conduit article list, 16 deep, with its timestamps decoded and its tag array walked;
- an INSERT.

Both sides use the same prepared statements, parameters and pipeline depth, fold every column into a checksum, and must print the same rows. Both binaries are built with the hardening scripts/build.sh applies, read from build.sh itself, and both pass scripts/check-hardening.mjs.

The two sides don't exchange the same messages. libpq's PQsendQueryPrepared sends Describe Portal on every query, and parses the RowDescription it gets back. nish/net/postgres describes once, at prepare, and sends Bind, Execute and Sync. The README counts the messages, bytes and system calls per query on each side (strace), with user and system time split, and callgrind instructions.

Under the protocol's rule (the median, claimed only beyond the build-against-copy band and both sides' IQR):
- pk: −1.26 µs (−15.1 %), mostly user-space instructions;
- list: −9.39 µs (−21.6 %), of which instructions explain about 2 to 3 µs at each side's own rate and kernel time some more, and the rest is stated as unexplained;
- insert: not claimed, since it falls inside libpq's spread and moves between sessions.

The module itself got faster on the way: `next` and `readRow` read lengths inline, and `timestamp` checks its shape in one pass. Its answers are unchanged, which new tests pin: lengths with the top or high bit set, a 258-byte column, and misshapen timestamps. callgrind puts list at 133,970 → 114,919 instructions a query (−14 %), though the clock can't see it.

Setting TCP_NODELAY by hand mattered more than any of that. `tcpConnect` leaves Nagle on, which cost the Nish side about 45 % on pk, all of it system time (#942).
